WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.443
JENNY BROWN: When it comes
to gaming, whether it's

00:00:02.443 --> 00:00:05.280
action, RPG or simulation,
staying in the action

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:06.970
means being online.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.110
But in the offline world, you
rep your fandom with merch.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.090
Will Critter Junction's
e-commerce shop

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:13.560
be able to handle their hype?

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:14.890
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.882
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.100
Without any
in-person conventions

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.830
this year, Critter Junction
players from around the world

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.260
have begun to flood their
site to purchase character

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.170
cards, apparel, and other swag.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.960
Critter Junction has been
testing App Engine standard

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.120
to run their new
merchandise shop,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.550
Because App Engine auto
scaled their application

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.040
across multiple instances
to meet the demands

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.780
of additional traffic.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.360
But during load testing,
they used Cloud Trace

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.190
to measure response latency.

00:00:48.190 --> 00:00:50.440
And they noticed a
higher than usual latency

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.780
when they used heavier
concurrent requests

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:55.840
to their service's
HTTP end point.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.510
You see, pending
latency is how long

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.610
a request can be
sitting in the queue

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:04.310
before App Engine decides
to spin up another instance.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.720
If all of your app instances
are busy when a request arrives,

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:09.850
the request will wait
in a queue to be handled

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.070
by the next available instance.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.680
As the load increases,
this means requests

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.080
are processed more slowly.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.270
App Engine will then
start a new instance

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.020
to help based on limits you
set, like CPU utilization,

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.270
throughput, and max
concurrent requests

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:27.680
of the current
running instances.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.250
But what they didn't know
is that App Engine also

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:34.210
needs to load their app's code
into a fresh instance when they

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.680
redeploy a new
version of their app,

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.850
and when maintenance and repairs
of underlying infrastructure

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:41.680
or physical hardware occur.

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:44.680
Though cold starts on
App Engine are rare,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.550
the first request or loading
request sent to a new instance

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.030
can take longer to be processed,
because the instance first

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:54.220
has to load your
app's code, including

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:58.340
any libraries and resources
needed to handle the request.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.120
This means a gradual
increase in response times

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.130
to handle new traffic.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.800
What you want instead is
to get the initialization

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.610
to happen sooner, before a new
instance serves live traffic.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.100
You can do this by using
a warmup request, which

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.160
loads application code into
an instance ahead of time,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.530
before any live
requests reach it.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.180
App Engine attempts
to detect when

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.940
your app needs a new instance
and initiates a warmup

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.070
request to initialize it.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.170
Any new instances
accept requests

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.750
after they finish
loading your app's code.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:31.210
New requests can then
be handled faster.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:34.210
Warmup requests are used by
the App Engine scheduler, which

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.250
controls auto-scaling
of instances

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.710
based on your configuration.

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:42.190
App Engine issues get
requests to /_ah/warmup.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.220
And then you can implement
handlers for this request

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:48.460
in your code to perform
application-specific tasks,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.560
like pre-caching app data.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.830
For most supported languages,
just add the warmup element

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:55.930
under the inbound
services directive

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.270
in your app.yaml file.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.910
Then create a handler
that will process requests

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.250
that are sent to ah.warmup.

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.590
Your handler should
perform any warmup logic

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:07.670
that is needed by your app.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.860
Let's walk through a Go example.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.280
Our function performs
the required setup steps

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.860
for the application
to function, and it

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.950
logs when an App Engine
warmup request is used

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:19.900
to create the new instance.

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:22.780
These warmup steps happen
in setup for consistency

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.600
with cold start instances.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.770
Everything else
remains the same.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.600
This setup function executes
the per instance one-time warmup

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.150
and initialization actions.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.430
Finally, the index
handler responds

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:38.860
to requests with our greeting.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.990
One thing to note
is warmup requests

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:44.050
don't work in all cases,
despite the best-effort attempt

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.750
to send requests to
already warmed-up instances

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.160
when enabled.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.230
So you might still
face loading requests,

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.290
like if the instance is the
first one being started up

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.900
or if there's a steep
ramp-up in traffic.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.020
In these cases, you should use
the resident instances, which

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:01.000
you can learn about below.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.040
Once warmup requests
were implemented,

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:06.010
Critter Junction was able to
reduce cold start instances

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.590
during increases in traffic
to their online shop

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.870
once convention
season was underway.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.450
Stickers for everyone!

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.029
Check out the link below
for language-specific steps

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:18.490
on warmup requests, and
stay tuned for what's

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.019
next for Critter Junction.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:21.940
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

